t to an empty option  you can see that the number of remaining  options has dropped from 4 to 2. this  number will also represent the value of  entropy for a given tile  we now begin a recursive cycle of  observing and propagating in the  observation phase a random minimum  entropy tile is selected a selection is  randomly made from the remaining options  the tail option was chosen and remaining  options on the tile has dropped to 1. in  the propagation phase all the tiles  adjacent to the observed tile must  adjust their domain to satisfy the  constraints the propagation continues  recursively until no more tiles are  modified with every iteration more tiles  are collapsed down to a single option  the solve keeps iterating  finally the solve reaches a state where  the grid contains only collapsed tiles  here we can see this process in two  dimensions using a six by six grid  here we see the process in three  dimensions using a three by three by  three grid  a wave function collapse model is a data  asset that stores constraints a  constraint is stored in the form of a  key option an adjacency  and the adjacent option  defining constraints can be difficult  some implementations have used  label-based systems but that can be  confusing or complicated from an  artist's perspective  our implementation derives constraints  from arbitrary layouts  by doing so wave function collapse  models can be built visually and it may  help in discovering new options that are  required  you can simply specify a resolution of a  grid to solve  there is also an option to use a grid  actor which is a blueprint that visually  shows the bounding box of the grid one  advantage with the grid actor is that it  can derive starter options based on  static mesh actors that sit within the  grid on the left you can see a few tiles  pre-placed inside the grid actor and the  two different outputs on the right  it is a great way to combine art  direction with proceduralism  the city had so much comp